{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfUTwW4zDLYPMSMgxVbB2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a real strategic tradeoff, not a research task — let me give you a direct recommendation.\n\n**Delay the decision, but not passively — decouple it from the renewal.**\n\nThe core conflict: your biggest customer renews in 9 months and wants two specific features, but a Flutter rewrite would stall feature work for ~7 months across both platforms. Starting the migration now puts you in a position where you can't deliver those features until right around (or after) the renewal — that's a needless, high-stakes gamble on a relationship you can't afford to lose.\n\nA few things worth weighing explicitly:\n\n- **Sequence, don't choose blindly.** Ship the two features natively first (they're presumably scoped for the existing codebases anyway), secure the renewal, *then* revisit consolidation. A 7-month stall is much safer to absorb right after a renewal than right before one.\n- **The Flutter case doesn't weaken by waiting 9 months** — the cost of running two codebases (8 engineers instead of 5) keeps accruing either way, so delaying the *migration* isn't the same as delaying the *decision*. You can decide now that consolidation is the right long-term move and still sequence it after the renewal.\n- **The older-device stat is a real risk, not a footnote.** A third of users on older devices means you need to know *now*, not mid-migration, whether Flutter's rendering and plugin ecosystem cleanly support your minimum OS/device targets — 60K lines of native code per platform likely means deep platform-specific integrations (camera, background services, hardware) that are exactly where Flutter compatibility problems surface late. Spike this before committing, regardless of timing.\n- **\"Delay\" should have a trigger, not be indefinite.** Set a concrete re-evaluation point (e.g., \"after renewal signs\" or \"after the two features ship\"), otherwise this becomes permanent inertia and you keep paying for two teams with no plan.\n\nSo: commit to consolidation in principle if the economics support it (8 engineers → 5 is a real saving), but sequence the *migration itself* to start only after the renewal is secured — and use the next 9 months to spike the older-device compatibility question so you're not deciding blind when the window opens."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":40624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d860aa4a-2298-4db6-b1a7-e0d5b7ed4d9e","uuid":"4feb4b3c-165a-44a4-a754-2228234557ee","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:04:56.607Z","request_id":"req_011CfUTwVegftNJ2ew6ELJRM"}
